Were you paying attention, you'd have noticed that
the current spate of spam is using real, although
forged, author IDs, but travesty-generator-produced
article bodies, and you would have been able to
deduce that eventually such usage would coincide
with the authors normally posting to this newsgroup.

There's been at least one such forgery using Janis'
name here too, and one forged as if it came from
Jukka noticeable because it _wasn't_ set to
X-noarchive.

I happen to be a prolific Usenet poster, so my name
is being picked up and used often in forgings. I
find, monitoring my Google Groups profile, that I'm
currently being credited with authoring about six
times as many postings as I write myself, but I
rarely encounter them, since they go to newsgroups
in which I do not participate.

This spammer is both deranged and unoriginal, so
this series of postings is following a well known
pattern. In the future, as in the past, when the
forged author names are mostly from the appropriate
newsgroup, and the forged article bodies are mostly
reposts of ancient articles by those very authors,
or by other authors here, things are going to get
very  ugly indeed, as has been the case in past such
exercises.

Your best protection is to pay attention to the
Newsgroups and Followup-to lines of every posting
you read or (especially) to which you respond, and
see if it is targeted across newsgroups
inappropriate to the putative authorship, since the
_point_ of this exercise is to direct followups to
clutter a particular target newsgroup with which the
spammer is derangedly obsessed.

FYI

BTW, to add a data point, Google Groups _did_ let me
cancel that article supposedly from me, although,
since most News servers, due to rampant cancel
message forging, long ago stopped respecting cancel
messages sent to newsgroup "control", the main
effect of that cancel is restricted to the Google
Groups archives.

It's worth a comment that _any_ discussion of the
spamming (even this one) just adds noise to the
newsgroup, so everybody, try to resist the urge to
post "but _I_ didn't post that!" messages, or
criticisms of the spams as if they were written by
the authors in whose names they are forged, or
answering tis to tell me how right or wrong I am,
okay?

Another comment: using Google Groups to down-status
forged articles is just exactly the wrong approach,
it makes them look more like genuine articles.

Instead, at least until the spammer picks up the
trick, Google Groups users try assigning _some_
status to genuine articles, to make them stand out
from the crowd. This only helps other Google Groups
users, of course, a benefit of better Usenet tools.

Imitating this should be fairly challenging for the
spammer, since it requires _two_ interactions, using
different accounts, one to post the article, using
any news service, a second to flag the article
quality, only available via Google Groups once the
article is posted and available there.

Google Groups, as all of its users know, interrupts
users frequently with "prove you're not a 'bot"
windows, making the main spam attack infeasible from
that Usenet access provider.

